---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import DocPage from '../components/DocPage.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site.js';
import { projects, statusLabels } from '../data/projects.js';

const description =
    'Who the OpenVi Foundation is and why it exists: an independent, community-run group that maintains MIT-licensed forks of widely used front-end libraries.';

const contents = [
    { id: 'what-we-do', text: 'What we do' },
    { id: 'how-we-got-here', text: 'How we got here' },
    { id: 'who-is-behind-it', text: 'Who is behind it' },
    { id: 'how-we-approach-it', text: 'How we approach it' },
    { id: 'where-things-stand', text: 'Where things stand' },
    { id: 'contact', text: 'Contact' }
];
---

<Base title="About the OpenVi Foundation" description={description}
    image="/og/about.png"
    pageType="AboutPage"
    breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'About', path: '/about' }]}
>
    <DocPage
        title="About the foundation"
        lede="An independent organization that builds and maintains open source UI libraries for the web."
        label="About"
        contents={contents}
    >
        <h2 id="what-we-do">What we do</h2>
        <p>
            We maintain the parts of the front-end stack that applications sit on top of: component
            libraries, theming engines, icon sets and the tooling around them. Everything we publish
            is MIT licensed and developed in public.
        </p>
        <p>
            Maintenance is the actual product. Plenty of open source gets written; much less of it
            gets kept working across framework releases, browser changes and security advisories for
            years afterwards. That long tail is the work we take on.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-we-got-here">How we got here</h2>
        <p>
            The foundation formed around a fork of PrimeVue 4.5.5, the last PrimeVue release under
            the MIT license. PrimeVue 4 now receives security fixes only, and new development moved
            to PrimeVue 5 under a license that is not MIT. PrimeVue is one of the most widely adopted
            component libraries in the Vue ecosystem, and a very large number of applications on the
            MIT 4.x line had no MIT path for further development.
        </p>
        <p>
            When a dependency that central stops being maintained you have three options: freeze it
            and accumulate risk, rewrite your UI layer, or fork it and maintain the fork. We went
            with the third, in the open rather than in a private fork. The same reasoning applies to anything
            else we take on.
        </p>

        <h2 id="who-is-behind-it">Who is behind it</h2>
        <p>
            Engineers who know these libraries well. There is no company behind this and no funding.
        </p>
        <p>
            That changes what we care about. We are not chasing feature announcements or trying to
            build a brand. The goal is to keep a widely used dependency stable for the people who
            rely on it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-we-approach-it">How we approach it</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Compatibility over novelty.</strong> A fork keeps the API of the project it
                was forked from. Moving to it should be a dependency swap, not a rewrite.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Stability first, growth second.</strong> Backlog and dependency currency
                come before new components.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Own the full stack.</strong> We forked the theming engine and icon set too,
                so no critical piece resolves at runtime from a scope we do not control.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>MIT, permanently.</strong> Every release stays MIT. That is why the fork
                exists.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Boring release engineering.</strong> Predictable, versioned releases with a
                changelog you can actually read.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="where-things-stand">Where things stand</h2>
        <p>
            Every project is on a stable release and follows semantic versioning from here. Current
            work is the issue backlog before new surface area.
        </p>
        <ul>
            {
                projects.map((p) => (
                    <li>
                        <a href={`/projects/${p.slug}`}>{p.name}</a> <code>{p.version}</code>,{' '}
                        {statusLabels[p.status].label.toLowerCase()}. Forked from {p.forkedFrom}.
                    </li>
                ))
            }
        </ul>
        <p>
            Each project keeps its own plans. OpenVue's are on its <a
                href="/projects/openvue/roadmap">roadmap</a
            >, with every release in the <a href="/projects/openvue/changelog">changelog</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
        <p>
            Day-to-day conversation happens on <a href={site.discord} rel="noopener">Discord</a>. For
            anything else, email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> or open an issue on
            the relevant repository under <a href={site.github} rel="noopener"
                >github.com/openvi-foundation</a
            >. Security reports should follow the <a
                href="https://github.com/openvi-foundation/openvue/blob/master/SECURITY.md"
                rel="noopener">security policy</a
            > rather than a public issue.
        </p>

        <p class="small muted">
            OpenVi Foundation is not affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by PrimeTek
            Informatics. Every fork keeps PrimeTek's copyright notice under the MIT license the
            original code was released with. PrimeVue, PrimeIcons, PrimeUIX and Volt are names of
            their respective owners, used here only to describe where the code came from.
        </p>
    </DocPage>
</Base>
